WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.800
[Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.050
[Tavi sings an A, 440 Hz]

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.959
TAVI: Same note, surely?

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:11.762
MERC: Is it?
Mine shook the air 220 times a second.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.662
Yours shook it 440.

00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:18.538
TAVI: Twice as fast?
Then why do they sound the same?

00:00:18.938 --> 00:00:23.717
MERC: Good question. To answer it,
we need to know what a note really is.

00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:27.117
[chime]

00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:34.176
By the end, you'll know what a note really
is, why notes come back an octave higher,

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:37.317
and how to read names like A4 and C4.

00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:42.920
Every note you have ever heard
starts with something shaking.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.633
A guitar string.

00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:45.997
A speaker.

00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:48.609
The vocal folds in your throat.

00:00:48.709 --> 00:00:51.483
[a string is plucked]
Pluck a string, and it swings

00:00:51.566 --> 00:00:54.109
back and forth, pushing on the air.

00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:56.868
Here it is, slowed right down.

00:00:57.268 --> 00:01:01.718
Each push travels outward as a wave,
all the way to your ear.

00:01:02.118 --> 00:01:06.448
Count the swings in one second,
and you have the note's frequency.

00:01:07.048 --> 00:01:12.103
At real speed,
this string swings 220 times.

00:01:12.403 --> 00:01:15.647
We measure it in hertz. Swings per second.

00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:18.712
Slow swings make a low note.

00:01:18.795 --> 00:01:20.747
[a tone sweeps up from 110 to 880 Hz]

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.288
Fast swings make a high one.

00:01:23.888 --> 00:01:26.621
TAVI: So a high note is just a loud note?

00:01:26.921 --> 00:01:28.097
MERC: Not quite.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.630
[the tone gets louder]
Loud is how far it swings.

00:01:30.714 --> 00:01:32.714
[the tone goes higher]
High is how fast.

00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:36.363
MercuryPitch draws
your voice the same way.

00:01:36.446 --> 00:01:39.741
As you sing,
a line climbs when you go higher.

00:01:42.541 --> 00:01:44.509
Now, back to our puzzle.

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:46.952
Here is 220.

00:01:47.252 --> 00:01:49.337
And here is 440.

00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:52.026
Exactly twice as fast.

00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:58.265
Before you listen, predict.
Two different notes, or the same one?

00:01:58.565 --> 00:02:01.165
[pause and predict]

00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:02.765
[220 Hz]

00:02:03.065 --> 00:02:04.465
[440 Hz]

00:02:04.765 --> 00:02:06.765
[both together]

00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:11.355
Higher, yes. But together,
they melt into one note.

00:02:11.705 --> 00:02:13.284
That jump has a name.

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:14.834
An octave.

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:17.071
Now look at the waves.

00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:22.130
Every second peak of the fast one
lands right on a peak of the slow one.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.126
They line up again, and again, forever.

00:02:26.626 --> 00:02:31.324
TAVI: Like us walking together.
I take two steps for every one of yours.

00:02:31.624 --> 00:02:37.292
MERC: And we land together, every time.
Two for one: the simplest fit there is.

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:38.793
That's the octave.

00:02:39.243 --> 00:02:43.155
[220 and 277 Hz drift in and out]
Try 277 instead, and the peaks drift in

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:47.124
and out of step. That's a different note.

00:02:47.624 --> 00:02:50.498
Go down an octave, to 110.

00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:51.665
[A: 110 to 880 Hz]

00:02:51.748 --> 00:02:56.983
Then double, and double, and double.
The same letter, every time.

00:02:57.433 --> 00:03:02.873
The gaps in hertz keep doubling,
but every jump sounds the same size.

00:03:02.957 --> 00:03:06.079
Your ear hears multiplying, not adding.

00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:13.083
So pitch isn't really a straight line.
It's more like a spiral.

00:03:13.483 --> 00:03:17.863
Go up an octave, and you come back round,
right above where you started.

00:03:18.463 --> 00:03:22.950
Musicians name the notes on
each turn with seven letters.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.950
[eight notes: A B C D E F G A]

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.880
TAVI: Only seven notes?

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.630
MERC: Seven letters.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.110
Twelve notes.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.070
The other five are next time's story.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:42.033
A number tells you which turn you're on.
Each new turn starts at C.

00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:48.842
So the A that orchestras
tune to is A4. 440 Hz.

00:03:49.142 --> 00:03:55.124
Middle C on a piano is C4.
Same letters, different turns.

00:03:55.524 --> 00:03:59.284
[a run up the keys]
Unroll the spiral, and you get the piano.

00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:02.848
The same seven letters,
octave after octave.

00:04:03.848 --> 00:04:09.085
When two people sing the same tune,
they're often an octave apart. Listen.

00:04:09.385 --> 00:04:13.985
[a tune, two voices an octave apart]

00:04:14.335 --> 00:04:16.791
Two voices, one tune.

00:04:17.091 --> 00:04:21.570
So when you match a note,
an octave up or down still counts.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:28.203
Let's try. I'll sing an A. Sing it back,
or hum along, wherever it feels easy.

00:04:28.603 --> 00:04:30.803
[Merc sings an A]

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:34.403
[your turn: sing or hum an A]

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:36.603
[Tavi hums an A, an octave up]

00:04:36.853 --> 00:04:39.015
TAVI: I hummed it higher. Does that count?

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:44.520
MERC: Higher, but the same note.
That counts. Keep it gentle. Never push.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.431
Quick check.

00:04:46.731 --> 00:04:49.221
What makes a note higher?

00:04:49.521 --> 00:04:51.321
[think]

00:04:51.621 --> 00:04:54.349
Faster swings. A higher frequency.

00:04:54.949 --> 00:04:57.206
Double the frequency, and you get?

00:04:57.506 --> 00:04:59.306
[think]

00:04:59.606 --> 00:05:01.755
TAVI: The same note, one octave up!

00:05:02.355 --> 00:05:05.942
MERC: And why do A3 and A4 share a letter?

00:05:06.242 --> 00:05:08.042
[think]

00:05:08.342 --> 00:05:13.629
Their waves fit two to one.
Same spot on the spiral, one turn higher.

00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:15.545
Next time.

00:05:15.845 --> 00:05:20.252
Seven letters, but twelve notes.
Where do the other five come from?

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:22.302
TAVI: Between the letters, maybe?

00:05:22.385 --> 00:05:25.249
[chime]
